# Nautilo — developer and AI-assistant orientation

Nautilo is a multi-user AI workspace: Humans and Genies collaborate in Rooms,
delegate durable Tasks, use shared Memory and Artifacts, and work through
browser, Desktop, and Mobile clients. This file maps the current source and
execution boundaries. Read [`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) for working rules,
[`CONTRIBUTING.md`](CONTRIBUTING.md) for contribution scope, and
[`DOCS.md`](DOCS.md) for the public documentation index.

This orientation was reconciled with source on 2026-09-09. Package manifests,
entry points, tests, and workflows take precedence over historical notes or
planning IDs when establishing current behavior.

## Product surfaces

| Surface | Source | Execution boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Workbench | `apps/workbench`, `packages/workbench-components` | Canonical browser UI, built and served by the Nautilo server on its origin. |
| Desktop | `apps/desktop/electron` | Electron connects to the selected server's Workbench and adds local device authority, native UI, and managed execution. |
| Mobile and Mobile Web | `apps/mobile` | Expo/React Native client plus the Mobile Web export served by the server; shares typed API and realtime contracts. |
| Administrator CLI | `apps/cli` | Profile-aware server administration, Compose/hosting deployment, and recovery; signed standalone and ordinary package channels are distinct. |
| Headless Relay | `bin/nautilo-relay` | Independently operated consumer of the shared Relay library, advertising its own supported handlers. |
| Development tooling | `bin/nautilo-dev`, `dev/` | Source-workspace builds, named instances, qualification, and deterministic checks. |

The TUI product is retired. Desktop is a connect-to-server client: it does not
embed the server, Postgres, Logto, or migrations. Production Workbench loading
uses the paired server URL; a local `dist/workbench` smoke fixture does not
change that architecture. Do not build a second Desktop Workbench or restore
a separate Vite-server production path.

## Find the owner before changing behavior

| Concern | Main source owners |
| --- | --- |
| HTTP/WebSocket control plane, routes, composition | `packages/server` |
| Agent graph, routing, providers, tool catalogue | `packages/agent` |
| Durable task execution, jobs, scheduling | `packages/runtime` |
| Identity, capabilities, scope admission | `packages/trust`, `packages/security`, `packages/cli-auth` |
| Database schema, queries, generated migrations | `packages/db`; typed-query checks in `packages/query-invariants` |
| Client HTTP/realtime and shared data contracts | `packages/api-client`, `packages/realtime-client`, `packages/types` |
| Relay protocol, transport, cancellation, capability acknowledgements | `packages/relay` |
| Local filesystem grants and workstation policy | `packages/desktop-filesystem-grants`, `packages/workstation-profiles`, `packages/sandbox`, Desktop authorities |
| Computer Use semantics, private protocol, native execution | `packages/computer-use-contracts`, `packages/computer-use-host-protocol`, `packages/computer-use-host` |
| Encryption and device/content authority | `packages/lattice-crypto`, `packages/lattice-bridge`, `packages/encryption-invariants`, server compositions |
| Reflection and Memory organization | `packages/reflection`, `packages/reflection-bridge`, server/runtime compositions |
| Document editing and first-party apps | `packages/document-mutations`, `packages/first-party-apps`, `packages/office-core`, `packages/office-docs`, `packages/office-sheets`, `packages/office-slides` |
| External task runtimes | `packages/codex-app-server-host`, `packages/claude-agent-sdk-host`, `packages/acp-host` |
| Deployment, profiles, hosting | `packages/compose-lifecycle`, `packages/hosting`, `packages/railway-hosting`, `packages/deploy-config`, `deploy/`, `packaging/` |

These are navigation pointers, not permission to bypass the owning layer.
Trace a change from producer through admission, persistence, execution, and
consumer before choosing its implementation location.

## Identity, data, and execution authority

Logto authenticates Humans. Nautilo resolves canonical principals, membership,
capabilities, scopes, and current device admission. PIN/biometric confirmation
is consent for an operation; it does not replace authorization. Keep Room,
Human, Genie, Task, Namespace, Memory, and Artifact identities explicit across
routing and asynchronous work. Never infer an owner/default agent when the
request's actual identity is missing.

Protected data has device and Namespace authority beyond ordinary route
access. Use the canonical crypto/data-operation compositions and supported
mode policy. A browser admission, relay advertisement, cached grant, or
historical message representation is not a replacement for current authority.
Do not introduce plaintext fallbacks to make protected reads or writes succeed.
See [browser crypto compatibility](docs/crypto-browser-compatibility.md).

External model, agent, browser, and connected-app providers are adapters.
Nautilo retains canonical task identity, routing, admission, approval, Memory,
and result ownership. Provider credentials, browser session capabilities,
cookies, and raw protocol details do not belong in model-visible arguments or
ordinary logs.

## Desktop, Relay Host, and Computer Use Host

The Desktop execution path has separate process owners:

```text
Server / Agent admission
  -> private Relay Host / RelayClient (authenticated server WebSocket)
  -> Electron authority and fixed Desktop handlers
  -> managed Computer Use Host (for admitted Computer Use)
  -> attested Cua Driver
```

The private Relay Host alone constructs Desktop's `RelayClient`. Electron
supervises that child over inherited pipes and keeps Human/session authority,
filesystem grants, permissions, browser surfaces, and local execution policy.
The headless Relay uses the same library independently; it does not acquire
Desktop-only capabilities by sharing that transport.

The managed Computer Use Host owns Cua supervision and provider parsing.
Electron supplies attested runtime paths and authorization bindings. Cua is
the native provider; the legacy Peekaboo handler is removed. Host release and
activation are independent of the Desktop release, while compatibility and
current grants still constrain every call. Preserve generation fencing,
verification, and cancellation: an uncertain mutation is not permission to
replay it through another process or provider.

Read the [Relay/Host ownership map](docs/relay-host-ownership.md) before changing
these boundaries. Keep context isolation, renderer sandboxing, sender checks,
fixed IPC contracts, and private inherited-pipe protocols intact.

## Tools, browsers, and connected applications

Model-facing tools belong in the Agent catalogue and typed contracts under
`packages/agent/src/tools`. Discovery does not grant execution authority;
server admission and the selected executor must still validate the call.
`dev/tools/` contains contributor harness personas/commands, not the product's
runtime tool registry. See [progressive tool activation](docs/progressive-tool-activation.md).

Connected Websites combine server-owned protected account/browser state with
supervised hosted or direct browser execution. They are distinct from local
Desktop browser control. Follow the [Connected Website contract](docs/connected-web-browser-contract.md)
for ownership, Human takeover, recovery, and credential containment.

Connected-app adapters, including OpenConnector, must preserve Nautilo-owned
Connection identity and typed outcomes. Do not treat an upstream MCP or API
surface as unrestricted tool authority. Semantic UI help is also separate:
[the Genie Application Bridge](docs/genie-application-bridge.md) keeps executable
routes and focus behavior in the installed client; remote catalogue updates
are metadata only.

## Office, Artifacts, and local files

Nautilo owns the Wafflebase-derived Core, Docs, Sheets and Slides source inside this workspace.
Build it through Bun/Turbo; do not require a Wafflebase submodule, upstream
backend, or external package release. `private` in these package manifests
means they are not npm publication targets. See the
[owned-engine maintenance and qualification record](docs/office-engines/README.md).
Writer uses the owned Docs browser and Node exports. Slides has a packaged
mini-app enabled on fresh installs; upgrades preserve the Human's preference.
Notes and Board remain separate work; upstream source alone does not establish
a delivered Nautilo app.

Writer, Sheets, Design, and Video integrate with Nautilo's app/document
lifecycle. Preserve exact Artifact/Workspace identity, live document versions,
draft recovery, and commit ownership when importing, exporting, or editing.
A provider filesystem path is not a Nautilo Workspace path. Current Folder and
Desktop local files require the selected device's grants and relay boundary.

OfficeCLI is a pinned document-processing executable used by Desktop and
Server. Both manifests must select the same release and identical shared
platform artifacts. Update their hashes, provenance, and notices together;
see [OfficeCLI provisioning](docs/officecli-provisioning.md) and
[apply-patch ownership](docs/apply-patch-runtime-boundary.md).

## Translation and localization work

Code and documentation are currently in English. Translation PRs are welcome
for UI/UX, documentation, onboarding, help, accessibility text, errors, and
other user-facing content. Follow the
[translation contribution guidance](CONTRIBUTING.md#translations-and-localization).

Plan broader localization as dedicated, maintainable work with an agreed
language and surface scope, complete user journeys, fluent-speaker review,
coverage tracking, and a named maintenance owner. Aim for broad coverage across
affected clients and documentation, including failure and recovery states.
Reuse the shared Workbench/Desktop UI and agree on reusable message resources
and fallback behavior before changing production code.

Keep the six README editions aligned with the English `README.md`, including
links, commands, and product claims. Update translations when the source
changes or visibly flag stale content; disclose coverage gaps and pending
language review. Translated READMEs do not establish application language
support. See [README maintenance](CONTRIBUTING.md#readme-maintenance) for source
hashes and verification.

## Build, test, and operate from source

Use the repository's Bun workspace and package scripts. Install with
`bun install --frozen-lockfile`; inspect the relevant package's `package.json`
and workflow for its build/runtime pin. The contributor Bun pin and the
container runtime pin can differ, so do not replace one based on the other.

Start with focused checks for the changed behavior. The full repository checks
are `bun run lint`, `bun run typecheck`, `bun run test:unit`, and
`bun run lint:unused`. The executable CI lanes live in
[`.github/workflows/ci.yml`](.github/workflows/ci.yml) and
[`dev/scripts/ci-gates.sh`](dev/scripts/ci-gates.sh); affected-package selection
and invariant checks also matter. Report skips, prerequisites, and exact results.

Unit tests must not require a live database, server, provider key, or external
service. Integration/packaged acceptance owns those dependencies separately.
Use named development instances and inspect their resources before changing
processes, databases, ports, or profiles. The default instance can hold real
user data; do not repurpose it as an empty fixture. Configuration comes through
`@nautilo/config`; secrets and per-instance state stay outside source control.

Change the Drizzle schema first and generate migrations with repository tools.
Use the typed query boundary and close owned database clients. Run
`bun run limits:check` when changing a production/operator limit, and investigate
the actual producer-to-consumer behavior before recording a policy decision.

## Releases and current records

Follow [`RELEASE.md`](RELEASE.md) for the independent Desktop, server, CLI,
Mobile, and Host channels. A successful merge/build is not proof of public
publication, CDN propagation, store availability, or installed adoption.
Update [`CHANGELOG.md`](CHANGELOG.md) for user-visible changes and
[`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md) when distribution changes.
Use source-owned release records and verified artifacts rather than historical
version examples.

Public GitHub issues/PRs and [contribution guidance](https://nautilo.ai/community/contribute)
define proposed work. No private roadmap or local maintainer path is needed to
understand this source tree. Report vulnerabilities through
[`SECURITY.md`](SECURITY.md), never through a public issue containing exploit or
credential details.
